I asked "how much do stats matter in Nethack after a certain point"; a
semi-abstract question covering a portion of the game that, while not
directly specified, is definitely 'Not the beginning'.

Your response led off with "When I roll a character...", mentions only
your own personal experiences with STR/CON, and further mentions that
you tend to die before Minetown -- ie. you rarely see anything _but_ the
beginning of the game.

Nothing personal, but really, your answer doesn't address the question;
generalizing it to the rest of the game isn't meaningful.

I qualified my question with "after a certain point" because it's patently
obvious that a character with STR 6 is going to have some trouble, and
that stats will matter for him.  (Or DEX 6, or CON 6, or INT/WIS 6 for a
caster.  CHA 6 won't cause you any problems.)

>> Hauling loot is the least important thing for a new character to worry
>> about.
> Least important, as compared to what other stat-related issue? What is the 
> most-important stat related issue?

"Not losing so much STR from poison before you find a unihorn that you
can no longer carry basic equipment."  But, then, that's the early game.

I don't think stats matter much once you reach the midgame, other than
as a token convenience from the perspective of STR/CON if you don't have
a bag of holding or GoP yet, or to get hungerless casting as a wizard.

>> Drop all that crap that looks like it's valuable but you can't
>> use yet; park it by the stairs on _each_ level if you're really low STR.
>> Focus on getting what you need to make it to the higher levels -- good
>> armor (AC0) and a decent weapon.
> Good advice, guess I will have to carry them to the stairs to drop them 
> off...Oh wait, that would be carrying it somewhere wouldn't it?

Intentionally missing the point.  It's not that you should never pick it
up, it's that yes, if you try to carry it _all_ around, you're going to
run into weight issues.  To solve this, drop them by the stairs on each
level before going down to a new level where you will find more loot.

You can come back and get it later; nothing will disturb it while you're
off level.

> In order to take your advice about focusing on Good AC (AC0), I will 
> probably have to wear armor. That's about 200-300 CC. A decent weapon also 
> requires an additional 50-100 CC.

Seriously, you're vigorously applying math to a non-mathematical issue.

>> Worrying about monsters wearing armor is pointless; just kill them.  Put
>> it this way: You have so many +hit bonuses as a player that the easiest
>> way to find mithril in the Mines is to find the dwarf you actually miss
>> occasionally.  Monsters putting on scale mail isn't going to save them
>> one bit, or even allow you to notice.
> This is a good point, and it brings to light something odd. If my main focus 
> is getting a low AC, then shouldn't that be the monster's focus as well? 

Greedy monsters will rush towards armor to pick it up, but unless you're
leaving +4 mithril lying around on the floor, it won't help them
significantly.  Try not to leave +4 mithril lying around; put it on,
instead.

> Me denying them that advantage should be a reasonable decision. But you're 
> saying it's not?

Correct.  Monsters do not get the huge to-hit bonuses the player has
available, whether that's from XL, DEX, weapon (the starting +1 matters),
weapon skill (Skilled matters), weapon type (Daggers get a bonus) or whatever 
else you have lying around.  Don't waste time trying to deny monsters
armor unless it's on the level of the +4 mithril mentioned above; you'll
hit 'em anyway.

Plus, most monsters in the early game start at AC10.  For them to get
armor equivalent to yours, they need to find a _lot_ of armor, and there
really just isn't that much lying around on the dungeon floor -- even
AFTER you run over all the dwarves in the Mines.

